          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Plato's \"Republic\"</strong><br>\nRepublic is Plato's best-known work, in which he describes a just society. He envisioned a society ruled by philosopher-kings, who would make decisions based on their wisdom and knowledge of the Forms. \"Republic\" is presented as a series of dialogues, mainly between Socrates and other characters, about justice, the just person, and the just city-state.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Allegory of the Cave</strong><br>\nIn \"Republic,\" Plato presents the Allegory of the Cave to illustrate how philosophers are like prisoners freed from a cave: initially blinded by the light (truth), they eventually come to see reality more clearly than those who never leave the cave's shadows. The allegory shows the philosopher's role in society as one who understands the true form of reality and thus guides others.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Plato's Works on Morality and Knowledge</strong><br>\nPlato also wrote extensively on morality and knowledge. His dialogues often feature Socrates in discussion with others, searching for definitions and understandings of various virtues. Through these dialogues, Plato explored the nature of virtue, the importance of knowledge for moral conduct, and the relationship between the soul and the body.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
