          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Influence on Western Philosophy and Christianity</strong><br>\nPlato's influence on Western philosophy cannot be overstated. His ideas have been foundational in the development of Western thought and Christian theology. His metaphysical and epistemological ideas influenced subsequent philosophers, shaping the intellectual trajectory of the West. The integration of Platonic ideas into Christian doctrine was particularly significant during the Middle Ages.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Critique of Athenian Democracy</strong><br>\nPlato was critical of Athenian democracy, which he saw as mob rule that led to the death of Socrates. He believed that the masses were too easily swayed by rhetoric rather than reason and that leadership should be based on wisdom rather than popularity. His political views thus favored an enlightened form of governance over the existing democratic model of his time.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Plato's Legacy and Death</strong><br>\nPlato died in 348 BCE, leaving behind a body of work that would influence philosophy for millennia. His approach to teaching through dialogues and dialectics continued in the philosophical traditions that followed. Even today, his work prompts discussions on ethics, politics, metaphysics, and epistemology. Plato's legacy lives on as educators and students worldwide continue to read and engage with his writings.</p>\n"
              },
